<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tartu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dorpat, Yuryev</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tartu is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, on the Emajõgi river. An Estonian hillfort there was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> around 1030 and named Yuryev, and in 1224 the German crusaders captured it, making it the seat of the Bishopric of Dorpat. The university founded by Gustav II Adolf in 1632 made it Estonia's intellectual centre, and the Treaty of Tartu of 1920 recognised Estonian independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tartu.jpg" alt="Tartu" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boreaallane · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tartu_(2019).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
